=begin pod

=TITLE App::FontSample

=SUBTITLE Create PDF samples of fonts

=head1 DESCRIPTION

C<App::FontSample> creates PDF font specimens, comparisons, collections, and
character charts.

The most important thing to know is that C<App::FontSample> does not provide
a collection of fonts. The library samples a font supplied as a
C<PDF::Content::FontObj>.

For a first test, use a PDF core font. No external font file or optional font
collection is required.

After that, applications may use OTF or TTF files, C<NotoFonts-OT>, or any
other source capable of supplying a suitable C<PDF::Content::FontObj>.

=head1 GETTING STARTED

=head2 1. Install App::FontSample

=begin code :lang<text>
zef install App::FontSample
=end code

=head2 2. Create your first sample

The following example uses the PDF core Times-Roman font. It is a good first
test because it does not require an external font file.

=begin code :lang<raku>
use PDF::API6;
use App::FontSample;

my $pdf = PDF::API6.new;
my $font = $pdf.core-font: :family<Times-Roman>;

create-font-sample(
    $font,
    :name<Times-Roman>,
    :output<sample.pdf>,
);
=end code

The resulting C<sample.pdf> contains a specimen of Times-Roman.

=head2 3. Try another font

C<App::FontSample> is provider-neutral. Once you have a
C<PDF::Content::FontObj>, the sampling code is the same regardless of where
the font came from.

For example, C<NotoFonts-OT> is an optional source of ready-to-use Noto font
objects:

=begin code :lang<text>
zef install NotoFonts-OT
=end code

Then:

=begin code :lang<raku>
use NotoFonts-OT;
use App::FontSample;

my $font = get-loaded-font('NotoSerif-Regular');

create-font-sample(
    $font,
    :name<NotoSerif-Regular>,
    :output<noto-serif.pdf>,
);
=end code

C<NotoFonts-OT> is not a dependency of C<App::FontSample>. It is simply one
convenient font source.

Some external font loaders require native system libraries. Install any
native prerequisites required by the font provider or loader you choose.

=head1 BASIC CONCEPT

The intended relationship is:

=begin code :lang<text>
font source
    |
    v
PDF::Content::FontObj
    |
    v
App::FontSample
    |
    v
PDF sample
=end code

Font providers are responsible for locating and loading fonts.
C<App::FontSample> is responsible for laying them out and creating the PDF.

=head1 LIBRARY API

=head2 create-font-sample

Creates a PDF sample from one C<PDF::Content::FontObj>.

A typical call is:

=begin code :lang<raku>
create-font-sample(
    $font,
    :name<MyFont-Regular>,
    :paper<Letter>,
    :layout<specimen>,
    :output<my-font.pdf>,
);
=end code

Important named options include:

=item C<name> - name displayed for the font; required
=item C<output> - output PDF pathname; required
=item C<paper> - C<Letter> or C<A4>; defaults to C<Letter>
=item C<landscape> - use landscape orientation
=item C<margin> - page margin in PDF points; defaults to 36
=item C<layout> - requested sample layout; defaults to C<specimen>
=item C<title> - document title
=item C<family> - optional font-family description
=item C<style> - optional font-style description
=item C<language> - optional language code for registered sample text
=item C<debug> - enable diagnostic messages

PDF dimensions are expressed in points; 72 points equal one inch.

=head2 create-font-collection-sample

Creates a PDF from a non-empty collection of
C<App::FontSample::FontEntry> objects.

For example:

=begin code :lang<raku>
use App::FontSample;
use App::FontSample::FontEntry;

my @entries;

@entries.push: App::FontSample::FontEntry.new(
    :name<Font-One>,
    :font($font-one),
);

@entries.push: App::FontSample::FontEntry.new(
    :name<Font-Two>,
    :font($font-two),
);

create-font-collection-sample(
    @entries,
    :layout<comparison>,
    :output<comparison.pdf>,
);
=end code

The default collection layout is C<comparison>.

=head1 LAYOUTS

=head2 specimen

Produces one specimen page per font. The page shows the font name, uppercase
and lowercase alphabets, numerals and punctuation, a pangram, and a size
waterfall.

=head2 waterfall

Currently accepted as a specimen-compatible layout. The specimen renderer
includes a size waterfall controlled by the C<sizes> option.

=head2 comparison

Shows the same sample text in each supplied font. Rows continue onto
additional pages when necessary. C<comparison-size> controls the sample size
and defaults to 18 points.

=head2 collection

Places a compact collection of fonts on one page. C<sample-size> defaults to
14 points and C<leading-ratio> defaults to 0.20.

The layout refuses to overflow the lower page margin. Use C<comparison> for a
font collection that does not fit on one page.

=head2 characters

Shows selected characters in a grid with a C<U+XXXX> label beneath each
glyph. The grid continues onto additional pages when necessary.

C<columns> defaults to 8. Library callers may also supply C<glyph-size>.

=head1 USING NOTOFONTS-OT FOR A COLLECTION

C<NotoFonts-OT> exports C<get-loaded-font>. A Noto collection can therefore
be assembled without a provider object:

=begin code :lang<raku>
use NotoFonts-OT;
use App::FontSample;
use App::FontSample::FontEntry;

my @entries;

for <
    NotoSerif-Regular
    NotoSerif-Bold
    NotoSans-Regular
    NotoSans-Bold
> -> $code {
    my $font = get-loaded-font($code);

    @entries.push: App::FontSample::FontEntry.new(
        :name($code),
        :$font,
    );
}

create-font-collection-sample(
    @entries,
    :layout<comparison>,
    :output<noto-comparison.pdf>,
);
=end code

=head1 COMMAND-LINE PROGRAM

The distribution installs C<font-sample>.

For direct use with an OTF or TTF file:

=begin code :lang<text>
font-sample \
    --font=/path/to/font.otf \
    --output=sample.pdf \
    --layout=specimen \
    --paper=Letter
=end code

Important command-line options include:

=item C<--layout=specimen|waterfall|comparison|characters|collection>
=item C<--paper=Letter|A4>
=item C<--landscape>
=item C<--margin=POINTS>
=item C<--language=CODE>
=item C<--text=TEXT>
=item C<--pangram=TEXT>
=item C<--sizes=N,N,...>
=item C<--characters=TEXT>
=item C<--columns=N>
=item C<--comparison-size=N>
=item C<--sample-size=N>
=item C<--leading-ratio=N>
=item C<--languages>

=head1 JSON INPUT

A JSON definition makes a set of samples reproducible.

Run a definition with:

=begin code :lang<text>
font-sample --config=font-samples.json
=end code

A single sample can be defined as:

=begin code :lang<json>
{
    "output": "output/sample.pdf",
    "layout": "specimen",
    "fonts": [
        {
            "file": "fonts/MyFont.otf",
            "name": "My Font"
        }
    ]
}
=end code

For several related outputs, use C<defaults> plus C<samples>:

=begin code :lang<json>
{
    "defaults": {
        "paper": "Letter",
        "margin": 36,
        "fonts": [
            {
                "file": "fonts/MyFont-Regular.otf",
                "name": "MyFont Regular"
            },
            {
                "file": "fonts/MyFont-Bold.otf",
                "name": "MyFont Bold"
            }
        ]
    },
    "samples": [
        {
            "output": "output/comparison.pdf",
            "layout": "comparison",
            "comparison-size": 18
        },
        {
            "output": "output/characters.pdf",
            "layout": "characters",
            "characters": "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789",
            "columns": 8
        }
    ]
}
=end code

Values in an individual sample override values in C<defaults>. Relative font
and output paths are interpreted relative to the JSON definition file rather
than the current working directory.

=head1 LANGUAGE SAMPLES

C<App::FontSample::SampleText> provides a small built-in pangram registry.

List the available language codes with:

=begin code :lang<text>
font-sample --languages
=end code

Library callers may register additional sample text at runtime.

=head1 TESTING

Run the normal test suite with:

=begin code :lang<text>
zef test .
=end code

The normal tests do not require C<NotoFonts-OT>.

Integration with C<NotoFonts-OT> can be tested separately by installing that
optional distribution and running the appropriate extended tests. This keeps
the core application independent of any particular font collection.

=head1 PROJECT STATUS AND FUTURE WORK

This release is intended to provide a useful working font sampler while
keeping the public interface independent of individual font providers.

Planned work is maintained separately in C<docs/TODO.rakudoc>.

=head1 SOURCE

The project source is maintained in the C<tbrowder/App-FontSample> repository
on GitHub.

=head1 AUTHOR

Thomas Browder

=end pod
